# The two halves of every claim's uncertainty, read together. One row per combination of what
# would make a range smaller and who owns the edge it would have to move, counted over the corpus.
#
# One instrument, two tables
#   `narrowsWhen` and `boundOrigin` are both required and typed on `Claim` because they work as one
#   instrument, and this is the pair the schema makes it possible to ask about. The ingest lines
#   them up on the claim they share, and two separate censuses never show where they cross. This is
#   the relation that joins them.
#
# Read the largest cell first
#   It is the one that should hurt. "Nobody has said what would narrow this" crossed with "nothing
#   sets this bound" is a claim with no course of action and no owner: a bare pair of numbers
#   dressed as a three-point estimate. Neither census alone can see it: in one it is an unremarkable
#   `unmeasured`, in the other an unremarkable `none`. It is the honest measure of how much of this
#   corpus is decoration.
#
# The small cells hold the advice
#   `instrument` against `contractual` says measure it and then go and renegotiate; `intervention`
#   against `intrinsic` says the bound does not move at all, so the only way out is to stop being
#   bound by that thing, which is exactly what the row filing it proposes, in prose.
#
# Corpus only
#   The fixtures file combinations on purpose to show the branches work, and counting a stipulation
#   as evidence of what people actually write is the error the whole scope/ directory exists to
#   prevent.
-- epistemics/widths.sqlc against epistemics/edges.sqlc, joined on the claim they share.
SELECT w.what_the_width_is_made_of AS what_would_narrow_it,
       e.who_owns_the_edge         AS who_owns_the_edge,
       count(*)                    AS claims
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/widths.sqlc)
) w
JOIN      (
    :compose(epistemics/edges.sqlc)
) e USING (filing, seq)
JOIN      (
    :compose(scope/corpus.sqlc)
) s USING (filing)
GROUP BY 1, 2
ORDER BY 3 DESC, 1, 2
